@inherits RazorSlice<StyleGuideViewModel>
@using Elastic.Documentation.Site.StyleGuide
@implements IUsesLayout<Elastic.Documentation.Site.StyleGuide._StyleGuideLayout, StyleGuideViewModel>
@functions {
	public StyleGuideViewModel LayoutModel => Model;

	private static readonly (string Key, string Label)[] Modes = [("light", "Light"), ("dark", "Dark"), ("black", "True black")];
}
<section class="sg-section">
	<h2>Light, dark and true black, side by side</h2>
	<p>Each panel pins its own mode with <code>data-theme</code>, so every theme is visible at once regardless of your system setting. True black is the dark roles on black, for OLED screens: surfaces step up in grey, and the accent and borders are re-tuned to hold their contrast. The theme switch, top right, picks light or dark, and its menu adds true black, contrast and motion, each with a match-system choice.</p>
	<div class="sg-themes">
		@foreach (var mode in Modes)
		{
			<div class="sg-themed" data-theme="@mode.Key">
				<h3>@mode.Label theme</h3>
				<p>Body text sits on the page colour. <span style="color: var(--color-text-muted)">Muted text is for secondary information.</span> <a href="#" style="color: var(--color-accent); text-decoration: underline">Links use the accent.</a></p>
				<div class="sg-swatches" style="margin-top: var(--space-4)">
					@foreach (var token in StyleGuideTokens.Surfaces.Concat(StyleGuideTokens.Status.Where(t => t.Name.EndsWith("surface"))))
					{
						<div class="sg-swatch">
							<div class="sg-swatch__color" style="--swatch: var(@token.Name)"></div>
							<div class="sg-swatch__meta"><span class="sg-swatch__name">@token.Name</span></div>
						</div>
					}
				</div>
			</div>
		}
	</div>
</section>
<section class="sg-section">
	<h2>How the default theme works</h2>
	<p>The Bold Minimal theme is one file, <code>Assets/ds/themes/bold-minimal.css</code>. Each colour role is declared once as a light and a dark value side by side, written as <code>var(--ds-l, light) var(--ds-d, dark)</code>, so the two modes can't drift apart and no browser needs <code>light-dark()</code>. The mode follows the system by default; setting <code>data-theme="light"</code>, <code>"dark"</code> or <code>"black"</code> on any element, usually <code>&lt;html&gt;</code>, overrides it for that subtree. Without JavaScript the page simply follows the system.</p>
</section>
<section class="sg-section">
	<h2>Writing your own theme</h2>
	<p>A theme only overrides tokens. Components read colour roles, never raw values, so redefining the roles restyles everything, and layer order lets your file win without <code>!important</code>. For a bolder redesign, override the scales (<code>--radius-*</code>, <code>--font-*</code>, <code>--shadow-*</code>) or add rules to the <code>ds.theme</code> layer.</p>
	<pre class="sg-themed" style="overflow-x: auto"><code>/* my-theme.css, loaded after ds.css */
@@layer ds.theme {
    :root {
        --color-accent: oklch(55% 0.2 300);
        --color-accent-hover: oklch(48% 0.2 300);
        --radius-md: 0;
        --shadow-slab: none;
    }
}</code></pre>
</section>
